  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that Triptersinin A may interact with Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase through a series of intermediate targets. Specifically, Triptersinin A is shown to target G2/mitotic-specific cyclin B1, which is itself a target of two different compounds: [(1S,2R,4aS,6aR,6aS,6bR,8aR,10S,12aR,14bS)-10-hydroxy-1,2,6a,6b,9,9,12a-heptamethyl-2,3,4,5,6,6a,7,8,8a,10,11,12,13,14b-tetradecahydro-1H-picen-4a-yl] formate and 1,1-Dimethyl-3-methylene-2-ethenylcyclohexane. Both of these compounds are known to target Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase. This indicates that Triptersinin A may act through a pathway involving these intermediates, potentially modulating the activity of Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase. The evidence is consistent with a mechanistic link between Triptersinin A and the enzyme, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be elucidated.",
